    <meta name="dc.description" content="Concerns regarding saturated fat intake have increased interest in low- and non-fat dairy products; however, milk fat removal from goat yogurt may impair texture and reduce fat-associated bioactives. Functional oil microparticles can restore these compounds while maintaining yogurt stability. In this study, wheat germ oil (WGO) microparticles prepared using octenyl succinic anhydride-modified starch (OSAS) and wheat gluten protein hydrolysate (WGPH) were investigated as functional fortifiers. Increasing WGPH content significantly enhanced water-holding capacity, oxidative stability, texture, and antioxidant activity (reaching 76.33%). Microstructural analysis revealed that the control yogurt had a porous protein network, whereas fortified samples, particularly those with WGPH, exhibited a denser and more compact structure. In vitro digestion indicated more controlled lipid digestion and higher &#945;-tocopherol bioaccessibility due to better interfacial stability from OSAS and WGPH. WGPH also enhanced umami taste. Overall, OSAS-WGPH microparticles improved structural, functional, and nutritional properties while negatively affecting sensory acceptability at high WGPH levels."/>
