    <meta property="og:url" content="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10068-026-02297-4"/>
    <meta property="og:type" content="article"/>
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="SpringerLink"/>
    <meta property="og:title" content="Enhancing the technological quality and α-tocopherol bioaccessibility of non-fat goat yogurt fortified with wheat germ oil microparticles stabilized by octenyl succinic anhydride-modified starch and wheat gluten protein hydrolysate - Food Science and Biotechnology"/>
    <meta property="og:description" content="Concerns regarding saturated fat intake have increased interest in low- and non-fat dairy products; however, milk fat removal from goat yogurt may impair texture and reduce fat-associated bioactives. Functional oil microparticles can restore these compounds while maintaining yogurt stability. In this study, wheat germ oil (WGO) microparticles prepared using octenyl succinic anhydride-modified starch (OSAS) and wheat gluten protein hydrolysate (WGPH) were investigated as functional fortifiers. Increasing WGPH content significantly enhanced water-holding capacity, oxidative stability, texture, and antioxidant activity (reaching 76.33%). Microstructural analysis revealed that the control yogurt had a porous protein network, whereas fortified samples, particularly those with WGPH, exhibited a denser and more compact structure. In vitro digestion indicated more controlled lipid digestion and higher &#945;-tocopherol bioaccessibility due to better interfacial stability from OSAS and WGPH. WGPH also enhanced umami taste. Overall, OSAS-WGPH microparticles improved structural, functional, and nutritional properties while negatively affecting sensory acceptability at high WGPH levels. Graphical Abstract"/>
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10068-026-02297-4/MediaObjects/10068_2026_2297_Figa_HTML.png"/>
    

        

