                <div data-article-body="true" data-track-component="article body" class="c-article-body">

                    
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Concerns regarding saturated fat intake have increased interest in low- and non-fat dairy products; however, milk fat removal from goat yogurt may impair texture and reduce fat-associated bioactives. Functional oil microparticles can restore these compounds while maintaining yogurt stability. In this study, wheat germ oil (WGO) microparticles prepared using octenyl succinic anhydride-modified starch (OSAS) and wheat gluten protein hydrolysate (WGPH) were investigated as functional fortifiers. Increasing WGPH content significantly enhanced water-holding capacity, oxidative stability, texture, and antioxidant activity (reaching 76.33%). Microstructural analysis revealed that the control yogurt had a porous protein network, whereas fortified samples, particularly those with WGPH, exhibited a denser and more compact structure. In vitro digestion indicated more controlled lipid digestion and higher α-tocopherol bioaccessibility due to better interfacial stability from OSAS and WGPH. WGPH also enhanced umami taste. Overall, OSAS-WGPH microparticles improved structural, functional, and nutritional properties while negatively affecting sensory acceptability at high WGPH levels.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" data-test="abstract-sub-heading">Graphical Abstract</h3>
<div class="c-article-section__figure" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure"><figure><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10068-026-02297-4/MediaObjects/10068_2026_2297_Figa_HTML.png?as=webp"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10068-026-02297-4/MediaObjects/10068_2026_2297_Figa_HTML.png" alt="" loading="lazy" width="685" height="277"></picture></div></div></figure></div></div></div></section><section aria-labelledby="Abs2" data-title="Highlights" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs2-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs2">Highlights</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs2-content">
            
            <ul class="u-list-style-bullet">
              <li>
                <p>OSAS-WGPH-based microparticles improved the technological and nutritional functionality of yogurt.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>YS10-P10 formulation showed the best WHC, stability, texture, and sensory quality.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>OSAS-WGPH microparticles enhanced lipid control and α-tocopherol bioaccessibility.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>Increasing WGPH in OSAS-WGPH microparticles enhanced antioxidant activity and umami perception but reduced sensory acceptance at high levels.</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>Microparticles altered yogurt color based on wall-material composition.</p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div></div></section>

                    

                    
    


                    
                        
                            
